    "en": {
      "title": "What a word class is",
      "summary": "A word class groups words that do the same job in a sentence.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A word class groups words that do the same job in a sentence. English has ten traditional classes: noun, pronoun, article, adjective, verb, adverb, preposition, conjunction, numeral and interjection."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**table** is a noun.",
                "**quickly** is an adverb.",
                "**and** is a conjunction."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How it works in English",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A word belongs to a class because of what it does in the sentence, not because of how it looks. Many English words belong to more than one class."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>One class</th><th>Another class</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>light</strong></td><td>a <strong>light</strong> bag (adjective)</td><td>turn on the <strong>light</strong> (noun)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>walk</strong></td><td>to <strong>walk</strong> (verb)</td><td>a <strong>walk</strong> (noun)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fast</strong></td><td>a <strong>fast</strong> car (adjective)</td><td>drive <strong>fast</strong> (adverb)</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
